      "text": "To solve the given alternating sum of integers, we group the terms in pairs. Each pair consists of two consecutive terms.\n\nStarting with the first term of the series, we can see how it relates to the next pair:\n- The first term of the series is \\(1\\).\n- The next term after \\(1\\) is \\(4\\).\n- The next combined pair is \\(1+4=5\\).\n\nContinuing this step, we form the pairs:\n\\[\n\\{1, 4\\}, \\{7, 10\\}, \\{13, 16\\}, \\ldots, \\{55, 52\\}\n\\]\n\nTo find the total number of such pairs and the total sum of each pair, we note that there are 26 pairs in total (since there are 26 integers from -46 to 55, grouped in threes).\n\nThe sum for each pair is:\n\\[\n\\{1, 4\\} = 1 + 4,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\{7, 10\\} = 7 + 10, \\ldots\n\\]\n\\[\n\\{55, 52\\} = 55 + 52.\n\\]\n\nEach sum of two consecutive terms in the series is:\n\\[\n5 + 10 = 15, \\quad 13 + 16, \\quad 21 + 24, \\ldots, 53 + 56.\n\\]\n\nNow compute the sum of the pairs:\n\\[\n5 + 10 + 13 + 16 + \\ldots + 52 + 55.\n\\]\n\nNote that each pair sums up to 15, and there are 26 such pairs. Therefore, the total sum of all these pairs is:\n\\[\n26 \\times 15.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\\[\n26 \\times 15 = 390.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of the given expression is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{390}.\n\\]",
